          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a clinician writes diabetes in one place and cataract in another, does the \"with\" convention connect them?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: no.** The \"with\" convention only links two conditions when the alphabetic index itself pairs them with the word \"with.\" Two separate mentions in a record do not trigger it.\n\nThe convention works like this. When the alphabetic index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification reads that as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the link, even if the record does not state it. The clearest example is type 2 diabetes with chronic kidney disease, which is coded as diabetic kidney disease unless the record says the two are unrelated (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*; *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*).\n\nThe same source warns that the presumption holds only for pairings the index actually lists. A condition that simply appears on the same problem list is not automatically a diabetic complication. So a diabetes entry and a cataract entry in separate places do not, by themselves, make a diabetic cataract.\n\n**What the retrieved material did not cover:** I searched for a diabetes-cataract pairing and the results did not include one. None of the passages say whether the index lists cataract under diabetes, and none give the code for it. The eye chapter was only mentioned in passing as a place where diabetic retinal detail is coded. So I cannot tell you from these sources how a diabetes-cataract combination would be coded. A medical coder or the classification's current index would need to answer that.\n\nIf this relates to a real record, a clinician or a certified coder can say whether the two conditions are linked in the record. Discuss any health concern with your own clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
